# Timestamp-based playback synchronization

Branch: audio-playback-safety, based on checkpoint/audio-sync-1.1.027.

Status: resumed audio-sync after the operator reported remaining drift with1.1.026.
Its saved three-hour common-clock session shows estimated lag growing from23ms
to approximately350ms, output queue33–38ms, and relative-rate correction clamped
at-250ppm. The operator authorized the timestamp-controller follow-up in this
branch. The earlier video-clock repair remains integrated; issue18 closure is
historical acceptance, not proof of long-session synchronization.

## Objective

Diagnose reported changing audio/video offset from macOS Host to Ubuntu Client,
then synchronize using source timestamps without increasing normal video latency.
Keep PLANK Output, exact video profiles, native transport, and input unchanged.

## Evidence and boundaries

- Native transport retains audio PTS in milliseconds and video PTS in 90 kHz
  ticks. The Client previously dropped audio PTS at its decoder submission
  boundary; the accepted change preserves it for measurement.
- macOS source audio/video share the Host monotonic clock. Linux audio currently
  starts an independent sample-count epoch; its video epoch is also independent.
  Never interpret these Linux timestamps as absolute audio/video alignment.
- The retained audio controller fits sample progress against rendered video.
  Its pending-audio backlog query always returns zero on the native path.
- SDL queued bytes describe unconsumed input; a device buffer is only an estimate
  of downstream output latency. Neither is a measured speaker presentation time.
- Existing short tone/coherence checks do not establish sustained lip sync.
- Confirmed renderer-ownership bug: EGL moves the AVFrame reference, resetting
  its PTS before Pacer publishes the video clock. The reported hardware path
  uses EGL. Snapshot PTS before rendering; preserve render-end timing and trace
  identity. This restores the existing correction reference without adding delay.

## Sequence

1. Read-only installed baseline, exact package versions, queue and timestamp
   evidence. No session interruption or changes to audio routing.
2. Preserve source PTS through Opus decoding, including bounded loss concealment,
   loss before the first packet, timestamp discontinuities and reconnect reset.
   Add rate-limited diagnostics comparing common-clock audio/video timestamps and
   estimated output delay. Preserve playback behavior for the baseline candidate.
3. Correct the confirmed lost video-clock publication and test both consuming and
   non-consuming renderers. Measure this minimal fix before changing the playback
   controller. If drift remains, use the measurement to locate capture, transport
   or presentation delay and replace sample-count correction with one timestamp-
   aware controller, not another loop on top. Define Linux's clock contract first.
4. Test initial skew, signed drift, jitter, loss, long gaps, device changes,
   idle video, reconnect and shutdown; retain bounded queues/cancellation.
5. Qualified builders produce branch-labelled candidates. A synchronized flash/
   click source and at least one-hour run must measure both changing offset and
   absolute offset; repeat with Linux Host before declaring shared-path acceptance.

## Acceptance

No growing offset, extra video delay, unbounded audio queue, silent timestamp
fallback, competing controllers or new user synchronization preference. Report
estimated output timing separately from actual acoustic/visual measurements.
The operator authorized integration after candidate testing. Deployment, release,
and interruption of an end-user session still require their separate authority.

## 1.1.027 controller

For common-clock macOS Hosts, use one phase controller fed by the same source
timestamp/output-delay observation as telemetry. Negative lead speeds audio up;
positive lead slows it. Filter every audio block (not subsampled video/device
jitter); update bounded resampling at100ms intervals, with a5ms deadband and
at most1% correction. No integral windup, new queue, video delay, or user setting.
Unknown/stale timing, large source discontinuities and pauses cancel compensation.
Output starvation/full-queue guards limit correction when phase is not physically
recoverable by resampling. This estimates output time; it is not a hardware clock.

Linux Hosts retain the existing relative-rate controller because their source
epochs are independent. Policies are mutually exclusive, never added together;
do not invent an absolute Linux phase. Remove the inactive backlog controller
and SDL pending-audio drop checks: the retired query always returns zero. Native
queue capacities, wire timestamps, capture, encoder and Host code stay unchanged.

Validation includes signed phase/clock drift, jitter aliasing, invalid timestamps,
output-pressure guards, tick wrap/reconnect and two-hour synthetic feedback, plus
ten-minute simulated output clocks driven by actual FFmpeg resampling. Repeat the
existing Linux rate-fit tests and EGL consuming-frame regression. Hosted builds
do not replace a multi-hour synchronized live flash/click test on either Client OS.

## 1.1.028 output-demand qualification

The operator authorized integrating027 as a rollback checkpoint before this
follow-up. Its real soak reports brief crackles despite bounded estimated phase.
Audio concealment was absent during one reported event; SDL input briefly empty
is supporting evidence, not proof of an audible underrun. Preserve this distinction.

Replace the fixed producer-side10ms starvation cutoff with a guard at SDL's
actual output-demand boundary. A callback records requested input, shortage
requests and remaining headroom under SDL's existing stream lock. It never
allocates, logs, waits for another lock, supplies silence, drops or buffers audio.
The decode worker snapshots it under the same lock; logging stays off the output
thread. SDL may overestimate required input, so counters are named shortage
requests, not measured speaker underruns. No new transport/statistical loss claim.

The existing phase controller stops acceleration if a pull leaves less than
one source block or reports insufficient input. Resume only after three distinct
consecutive pulls retain two blocks; stale/missing demand never permits catch-up.
This constrains the existing rate correction, not another controller. The phase
target, resampler, upper queue bound, video timing and Linux independent-epoch
policy remain unchanged. Do not insert an arbitrary timing bias or claim that
guarding an infeasible phase error makes it synchronized.

Qualification adds actual SDL/FFmpeg finite-queue tests with independent arrival
and consumption clocks, output chunk sizes, jitter and signed clock drift.
An infeasible phase case must protect playback without pretending to reach zero
offset. Existing constant-depth models remain useful for rate/phase arithmetic,
but cannot establish underrun safety. After build gates, require another real
listening/flash-click soak: no crackles, no growing offset, acceptable absolute
sync and unchanged video latency. No automatic end-user installation.
